The oxygen evolution reaction (OER) plays a vital role in many alternative energy conversion technologies.[1] Because of the sluggish kinetics caused by multistep four-electron transfering associated with O-H breaking and O-O formation, OER requires efficient electrocatalysts to overcome kinetic barriers and expedite the reaction.[2] Over the past decades, owing to the superior catalytic performance, a number of bimetallic electrocatalysts for OER have been evaluated, including bimetallic oxides,[3] hydroxides,[4] phosphides,[5] and chalcogenides[6]. However, numerous bimetallic electrocatalysts are nanocomposites, whose performances are usually affected by many factors, such as particle size, morphology and synergistic effect.[3b, 7] Moreover, the mutual impact between the two kinds of metal remains elusive. In particular for some electrocatalysts with complicated components and poor crystallinity, the real phase cannot be precisely characterized, hampering the cross-validation between theoretical models and experimental results. These problems hinder the exploration and development of novel bimetallic electrocatalysts.[8] Consequently, defined structure and inter-metal interaction of a bimetallic electrocatalyst are essential for deciphering its performance improvement in OER.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), as a typical porous and crystalline materials composed by metal ions (or clusters) and organic linkers, have been widely investigated in recent years and proposed as potential catalysts.[9] MOFs can be designed and tuned at the molecular level, whose structure and coordination environment can be precisely determined by crystal X-ray crystallography. It is conducive to the theoretical prediction of electronic coupling and other interactions, thus facilitating the investigation of the reaction mechanism.[9b, 10] However, some MOFs are highly unstable when used as catalysts in certain reactions, such as hydrogen evolution reaction (HER), OER, and oxygen reduction reaction (ORR).[11] To address chemical stability problems of MOFs, multiple approaches have been explored, including those using the high-valence metal ions and metal-ion metathesis.[12] When carboxylates serve as the ligands, high-valent metal ions (e.g., $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ , $A|^{3+}$ , $Z r^{4+})$ are usually employed to strengthen the electrostatic interaction between the metal nodes and organic linkers.[13] Among the high-valent metal ions, Fe-based compounds (e.g., hydroxides, phosphides, nitrides) are widely investigated,[14] and utilization of some Febased MOFs as electrocatalyst for OER were also reported recently.[15] These investigations indicate that Fe-based MOFs are stable and active for OER. Therefore, Fe-based MOFs is a category of promising candidate for electrocatalysts compared with other classes of stable MOFs. If an iron atom in trinuclear Fe-cluster is replaced by a second atom, the structure of the resulting $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{M}$ clusters will be similar to that of bimetallic electrocatalysts (e.g., bimetallic oxides/phosphides/sulfides). Thus, the $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{M}$ clusters can mimic bimetallic electrocatalysts and serve as the nodes that bridge organic ligands. Bimetallic MOFs produced by immobilizing the $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{M}$ clusters in MOFs are expected to possess defined structure on the molecular scale, and thus are suited for studying the OER performance of bimetallic electrocatalysts in MOF system.
In this work, we designed and synthesized a series of MOFs based on $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{M}(\mu_{3}{-}\mathsf{O})(\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{C O O})_{6}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{3}$ $F e_{2}M$ , ${M=F e}$ , Co, Ni, $Z_{\mathsf{n}})$ clusters with the following considerations: (1) an iron atom in $\mathsf{F e}_{3}$ cluster can be replaced by a second metal with a similar radius that allows formation of $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{M}$ clusters,[13a, 16] which is beneficial to realize the performance comparison of bimetallic electrocatalysts; (2) the preformed $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{M}$ clusters are soluble and can maintain their structure and composition, which guarantees the formation of desired bimetallic MOF-based catalysts; (3) the resulting bimetallic Fe2M-MOFs feature enhanced chemical stability, satisfying the requirements of OER electrocatalysts. Accordingly, by bridging preformed $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{M}$ clusters with biphenyl3,4',5-tricarboxylic acid (BPTC) ligand, we prepared four isostructural $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{M}$ -BPTC MOFs (i.e., $\mathsf{F e}_{3}$ -BPTC (NNU-21), $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{C o}$ -BPTC (NNU-22), $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{N i}$ -BPTC (NNU-23) and $\mathsf{F e}_{2}Z_{\mathsf{n}}.$ BPTC (NNU-24)). Among these stable MOFs, NNU-23 exhibits the best OER performance with an overpotential of $365~\mathsf{m V}$ at $10\mathsf{m A}$ $\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ in 0.1 M KOH, outperforming most previously reported MOF-based OER electrocatalysts. The experimental results reveal that the bimetallic MOFs possess the better OER activity than monometallic MOFs. In addition, the theoretical computation demonstrates that introduction of the second metal can make the d-band center close to the Fermi level and form a stronger binding interaction between the adsorbates and catalysts, thereby expediting OER process. This investigation offers thought for establishing precise bimetallic electrocatalysts and exploring the effect of the second metal on OER.
NNU-21\~24 were synthesized by the reaction between preformed $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{M}$ clusters and BPTC ligands under solvothermal condition, with acetic acid served as the competing reagent (See Experimental Section in the Supporting Information). The optical microscope images (Figure S3) and SEM images (Figure S4) clearly show the similar morphologies of NNU-21\~24. X-ray crystallographic analysis reveals that NNU-21\~24 are isostructural and crystallized in the tetragonal system with $P4_{1}2_{1}2$ space group (NNU-21\~23) and $P4_{3}2_{1}2$ space group (NNU-24), respectively (Table S1). The preformed $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{M}$ clusters contain two Fe (III) ions, one M (II) ion and one $\mu_{3}–0$ and are connected with six $C H_{3}C O O^{-}$ groups and three $H_{2}O$ molecules (Figure S1).[13a] In NNU-21\~24, the six $C H_{3}C O O^{-}$ groups of the clusters are completely substituted by COO- groups from BPTC ligands, and each BPTC ligand connects three $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{M}$ clusters (Figure S2). Such a connection pattern is repeated to construct the 3D frameworks (Figure 1a) and 3D channels (exemplified by NNU-23 in Figure 1b). In addition, topological analysis using TOPOS software suggests that NNU-21\~24 exhibit a $^{3,6-c}$ net topology (denoted as $\{6^{11}{\cdot}8^{4}\}\{6^{3}\}^{2}$ using Schläfli symbol).[17] Figure 1c shows the natural tilling of NNU-23. Calculation by PLATON software reveals that the free volumes of NNU-21\~24 are $56.9\%$ , $56.6\%$ , $56.6\%$ and $56.7\%$ , respectively.[18]
The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of the assynthesized NNU-21\~24 agree well with the simulated pattern derived from single crystal X-ray diffraction (Figure 2a), demonstrating their good crystallinity and high phase purity. To assess the chemical stability of these MOFs in electrolyte solution, we immersed $50~\mathsf{m g}$ crystals in $20~\mathrm{mL}~0.1$ M KOH at room temperature. After $24\ h$ , the pH of KOH solution changed slightly from 13.00 to 12.95, and the PXRD patterns (Figure 2b) remain unchanged. These results confirm the excellent stability of NNU-21\~24 in $_{0.1}$ M KOH, and promise the utilization of these MOFs as OER electrocatalysts in alkaline medium. For $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{M}$ ( $\scriptstyle M=C0$ , Ni, Zn) clusters, previous reports determined the $\mathsf{F e/M}$ stoichiometry as 2:1.[13a] To verify the stoichiometry of $\mathsf{F e/M}$ in NNU-21\~24, we performed both energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) (Figure S6, Table S2) and inductively coupled plasma atomic emission spectroscopy (ICP-AES) (Table S3). The results prove the 2:1 stoichiometry of $\mathsf{F e/M}$ in NNU-22\~24. Moreover, the $^{57}\mathsf{F e}$ Mossbauer spectra of Fe2M
BPTC MOFs agree with that of $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{M}$ cluster (Figure 2c), indicating that the composition of $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{M}$ clusters is preserved upon formation of MOFs.
The thermal stability of NNU-21\~24 was evaluated by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) under $\O_{2}$ atmosphere, which reveals that NNU-21\~24 remain stable at ca. $350^{\circ}\mathsf C$ (Figure S7). The textural porosity was determined by ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorptiondesorption isotherms, where all the curves exhibit the typical type-I isotherms with a sharp nitrogen uptake at low relative pressure (Figure S8), evidencing microporous characteristics of NNU-21\~24. After ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption measurement, the images of NNU-21\~24 (Figure S10) are almost unchanged compared with those of as-synthesized MOFs. Notably, the ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms exhibit no pronounced difference before and after stability test in 0.1 M KOH (Figure S11). These results further indicate the excellent stability of NNU-21\~24. The water vapor adsorption isotherms measured at $298~\mathsf{K}$ (Figure S12) suggests that the channels of NNU-21\~24 can adsorb water vapor, which may contribute to the full contacts between active sites and electrolyte solution.
The electrocatalytic activity of NNU-21\~24 towards OER under 0.1 M KOH was evaluated using a typical three-electrode system, where carbon cloth (CC) was modified with catalysts by drop-casting method and used as the working electrode directly (See Experimental Section in the Supporting Information). Acetylene black (AB) was introduced to mix with the assynthesized MOFs to improve the conductivity. The optimal mass ratio between MOFs and AB and the appropriate loading of catalysts were determined according to NNU-23 (Figure S13 and Figure S14).[19] The optimal mass ratio and loading (i.e., 1:1 and $1\ m g\ c m^{-2})$ are then used in the following experiments. For comparison, commercial $\mathsf{I r O}_{2}$ was also examined under the same condition. According to the linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves (Figure 3a), NNU-22, NNU-23 and NNU-24 exhibit more negative potential and higher current density in comparison with NNU-21. The Tafel slopes derived from the LSV curves are also analyzed in Figure 3b. The corresponding Tafel slopes for $\mathsf{I r O}_{2}$ , NNU-21, NNU-22, NNU-23, NNU-24 are
83.0 mV dec-1, 122.7 mV dec-1, 77.2 mV dec-1, 81.8 mV dec-1 and $121.8~\mathrm{\mV}$ dec-1, respectively, demonstrating the more effective kinetics of bimetallic electrocatalysts in OER.
Furthermore, the electrocatalytic performance of MOFs was compared by recording their overpotential at a current density of 10 mA cm-2 ( η10).[20] As presented in Figure 3c, NNU-23 displays an overpotential of $365~\mathsf{m V}$ , which is lower than those of NNU-21 $(555~\mathsf{m V})$ , NNU-22 $(376~\mathsf{m V})$ ), NNU-24 $(522~\mathsf{m V})$ and $\mathsf{I r O}_{2}$ $(390~\mathsf{m V})$ ). Besides, the overpotential of NNU-23 is also one of the best among the reported MOF-based electrocatalysts in 0.1 M KOH (Table S4). We also calculated the electrochemical double-layer capacitance $\big(\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{d l}}\big)$ by measuring cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves to evaluate the electrochemical active surface area (ECSA).[21] Clearly, NNU-23 has the largest $\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{d}}\mathsf{1}$ $(5.10\ m\mathsf{F\ c m^{-2}})$ , and the $\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{d}}$ values of bimetallic MOFs (i.e., NNU-22 $(3.35~\mathsf{m F}$ $\mathsf{c m}^{-2},$ ), NNU-23 $(5.10\ m\mathsf{F}\ \mathsf{c m^{-2}})$ and NNU-24 $(2.69~\mathsf{m F}~\mathsf{c m^{-2}})$ ) are superior to that of monometallic MOF (i.e., NNU-21 (2.48 mF cm2)) (Figure S15 and Figure 3d). The $\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{d}\mathsf{I}}$ results suggest that bimetallic $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{M}$ MOFs have larger ECSA than monometallic $\mathsf{F e}_{3}$ MOFs when used as alkaline OER electrocatalysts. CV curves measured with a scan rate of $5\mathrm{\mV/s}$ under a wider  potential window are displayed in Figure S16. To study the electrode reaction kinetics, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) of different samples were performed at the potential of $1.6\ V$ vs. RHE.[22] As revealed by the Nyquist plots (Figure S17a) of the catalysts and corresponding equivalent circuit model (Figure S17b), the charge transfer resistance $(\mathsf{R}_{\mathsf{c t}})$ of NNU-22 (8.48 Ω), NNU-23 (4.183 Ω) and NNU-24 $(68.75\Omega)$ are significantly lower than that of NNU-21 $(77.45\ \Omega)$ , indicating the fastest charge transfer rate on the bimetallic $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{M}$ MOFs, that results in the enhancement of the electrocatalytic performance for OER.
The long-term stability is another crucial criterion to evaluate electrocatalysts for applicability.[23] For NNU-21\~24, the stability is assessed with CV and chronopotentiometric curves. As shown in Figure S18, all the subsequrnt LSV curves of the catalysts exhibit nearly no difference compared with the initial one after 2000 cycles. The chronopotentiometric curves (Figure S19) also keep unchanged without degradation up to $15\mathrm{~h~}$ . These results demonstrate the high stability of the as-synthesized NNU-21\~24 during the long-term OER process.
To further explore the enhanced OER performance of various bimetallic MOFs, we performed spin-polarized density functional theory (DFT) calculations, which are represented by the simplified $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{M}$ clusters (Figure 4a). As a benchmark, the Gibbs free energy (G) diagrams of OER for the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}$ cluster, which can be determined using the adsorption free energies of $0^{\star}$ , OH, $\mathsf{O H}^{\star}$ intermediate species, were firstly plotted by employing the computational hydrogen electrode (CHE) model (Further computational details are described in Supporting Information). As shown in Figure 4b, the rate-determining step (RDS) of Fe3 cluster is the conversion of $O H^{\star}$ to $0^{\star}$ (with a considerably high overpotential of $\boldsymbol{1.03\ V}$ , which is essentially caused by the weak adsorption of $0^{\star}$ intermediate on Fe site $(\Delta\mathsf{G}_{\mathsf{O}^{\star}}=3.16\ \mathsf{e V},$ ). Therefore, it is expected that the overpotential can be lowered by strengthening the $0^{\star}$ adsorption.
Next, we examined the effects of Co, Ni and Zn incorporation on OER activity of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}$ cluster. For each $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{M}$ cluster, both Fe and the doped M sites were considered and we found that Fe atoms show better OER catalytic activity than the second M atoms for all three systems. Therefore, only the free energy curves of Fe sites were plotted in Figure 4 (those of M sites are plotted in Figure S20). The adsorption of $0^{\star}$ , OH, ${\mathsf{O O H}}^{\star}$ on Fe site of $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{M}$ clusters we calculated are all significantly enhanced compared to those of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}$ cluster (Figure S21, Table S5). The $\Delta G_{0^{\star}}$ of Fe site is significantly lowered to 2.15, 2.45, and $2.49\mathsf{e V}$ in $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{C o}$ , $\mathsf{F}_{\mathsf{e}2}\mathsf{N i}$ , and $\mathsf{F e}_{2}Z_{\mathsf{n}}$ , respectively. In particular, due to relatively weak adsorption of $\mathsf{O O H}^{\star}$ species, the RDS of $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{C o}$ cluster becomes the conversion of $0^{\star}$ to $\mathsf{O O H}^{\star}$ . Overall, the OER overpotential of $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{C o}$ (0.41 V), $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{N i}$ (0.42 V), and $\mathsf{F e}_{2}Z_{\mathsf{n}}$ (0.59 V) clusters are significantly lower than that of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}$ cluster. Our calculations show that bimetallic clusters exhibit a pronouncedly improved OER catalytic performance, which is in good agreement with the experimental measurements.
To get some deep insights, we also plotted the partial density of states of metal atoms of various $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{M}$ clusters. The position of d-band center for each cluster is also labeled. As shown in Figure 4c, the d-band center of $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{C o}$ , $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{N i}$ , and $\mathsf{F e}_{2}Z_{\mathsf{n}}$ cluster are closer to the Fermi level than that of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}$ cluster. According to the d-band theory, a higher lying of d-band center would lead to stronger binding interaction between the adsorbates and catalyst, which essentially contributes to the enhancement of OER activity of $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{M}$ clusters.
We also employed turnover frequency (TOF) to evaluate the intrinsic activity of NNU-21\~24 based on the theoretical calculation results. Here, both Fe and M atom are regarded as active sites in NNU-21\~24. The plots of TOF vs. potential for NNU-21\~24 are shown in Figure S22a. With an overpotential of $400~\mathsf{m V}$ applied (Figure S22b), the TOF value of NNU-23 is the highest among NNU-21\~24. Besides, the TOF values of NNU-22 $(0.028\ \mathsf{s}^{-1})$ , NNU-23 $(0.030\ \mathsf{s}^{-1},$ and NNU-24 $(0.004\ \mathsf{s}^{-1})$ are apparently higher than that of NNU-21 $(0.002~{\mathsf{s}}^{-1})$ , further confirming the excellent OER performance of the bimetallic catalysts.
In summary, we proposed a model of bimetallic electrocatalysts affecting their OER performance through both experimental results and theoretical computation utilizing the structural similarity between $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{M}$ clusters and bimetallic electrocatalysts. We designed and synthesized a series of MOFs and employed them as electrochemical catalysts for OER. Among these MOFs, NNU-23 exhibits the best OER performance with an overpotential of $365~\mathsf{m V}$ at $10\ m A\ \mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ , outperforming most previously reported MOF-based OER electrocatalysts in 0.1 M KOH. Notably, all examined bimetallic MOFs present better OER performance compared with monometallic MOFs. The DFT calculation reveals that the second metal can result in the d-band center close to Fermi level, and form a stronger binding interaction between the adsorbates and catalysts, thus improving the OER performance.
